=\\sum_xp_X(x)\\,p_Y(t-x)$ for independent $X$ and $Y$, what does the term for one value $x$ represent?","options":["The probability that $X=x$ or $Y=t-x$","The conditional probability $\\mathbb{P}(X+Y=t\\mid X=x)$","The probability that $X=x$ and $Y=t-x$","The probability that $X+Y\\le t$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2bace1146f041cc1e4d9532c","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_convolution_a8782e74_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For independent $X\\sim\\operatorname{Exp}(2)$ and $Y\\sim\\operatorname{Unif}(0,1)$ and a fixed $t>1$, on which range of $x$ is $f_X(x)f_Y(t-x)$ nonzero?","options":["$0<x<t$","$0<x<\\infty$","$0<x<1$","$t-1<x<t$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ad28bb507c8b0240ae60d59c","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_convolution_a8782e74_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $X\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,1)$ and $Y=-X$. Why does convolving their marginal densities give the wrong distribution for $X+Y$?","options":["Equal marginals imply $X+Y\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,2)$","Convolution assumes independence, but $X+Y=0$","Negative covariance gives $f_{X+Y}=f_X-f_Y$","Opposite signs give $f_{X+Y}(t)=f_X(t)f_Y(-t)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"53bc770b3e1bd6d78b619410","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_convolution_a8782e74_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"$X$ is uniform on $\\{0,1,2\\}$ and $Y$ is uniform on $\\{0,1\\}$, independently. What is $\\mathbb{P}(X+Y=2)$?","options":["$\\tfrac{1}{3}$","$\\tfrac{1}{6}$","$\\tfrac{2}{3}$","$\\tfrac{1}{2}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"20cf8f4b5770e7a8de5baf88","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_convolution_a8782e74_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Independent $X\\sim\\operatorname{Unif}(0,1)$ and $Y\\sim\\operatorname{Unif}(0,2)$ are added. Find the density of the sum at $1.5$ using convolution.","options":["$1$","$\\tfrac{1}{2}$","$\\tfrac{1}{4}$","$\\tfrac{3}{4}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c8ed26c4060eac828ce3c04c","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_convolution_a8782e74_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"$X\\sim\\operatorname{Exp}(1)$ and $Y\\sim\\operatorname{Exp}(2)$ are independent, with rates $1$ and $2$. Using $\\int_0^t\\mathrm{e}^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\mathrm{e}^{t}-1$, which convolution density does $X+Y$ have for $t>0$?","options":["$t\\mathrm{e}^{-t}$","$\\mathrm{e}^{-t}+2\\mathrm{e}^{-2t}$","$2(\\mathrm{e}^{-t}-\\mathrm{e}^{-2t})$","$2\\mathrm{e}^{-3t}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6ef918fcbd449db1da9bc13a","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_convolution_a8782e74_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"$X$ takes values $4,7,9$ with probabilities $\\tfrac{1}{5},\\tfrac{1}{2},\\tfrac{3}{10}$, respectively, and $Y=-3$ with probability $1$. Using discrete convolution, what is $\\mathbb{P}(X+Y=4)$?","options":["$\\tfrac{1}{2}$","$\\tfrac{1}{5}$","$\\tfrac{7}{10}$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ea5acb264fbc3870c225d569","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_convolution_a8782e74_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"$X_1,X_2,X_3$ are mutually independent, each taking $0$ with probability $\\tfrac{2}{3}$ and $1$ with probability $\\tfrac{1}{3}$. What is $\\mathbb{P}(X_1+X_2+X_3=1)$?","options":["$\\tfrac{4}{27}$","$\\tfrac{1}{3}$","$\\tfrac{2}{9}$","$\\tfrac{4}{9}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d60ba3bafbbe59f7ac2d804e","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_convolution_a8782e74_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For independent $X,Y$ with densities $f_X,f_Y$, why are $\\int_{\\mathbb{R}}f_X(x)f_Y(t-x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$ and $\\int_{\\mathbb{R}}f_Y(y)f_X(t-y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y$ equal?","options":["Because independence implies that $X$ and $Y$ have identical marginal densities","Because $y=t-x$ reverses the limits and gives $\\mathrm{d}x=-\\mathrm{d}y$","Because independence implies that both marginal densities are even functions","Because $y=t-x$ gives $\\mathrm{d}x=\\mathrm{d}y$ without reversing the limits"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e7c1eda00512c777302dc85f","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_convolution_a8782e74_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"$X$ and $Y$ are independent. Their densities are positive on $(0,1)$ and $(0,2)$, respectively, and zero elsewhere. For exactly which values of $t$ is the convolution integral $\\int_{\\mathbb{R}}f_X(x)f_Y(t-x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$ positive?","options":["$t\\in(0,2)$","$t\\in(1,2)$","$t\\in(0,3)$","$t\\in(0,1)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0fa9864f450ec50888c77b5b","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_convolution_a8782e74_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"$X\\sim\\operatorname{Bern}(\\tfrac{1}{2})$ and $Y\\sim\\operatorname{Unif}(0,1)$ are independent, and $f_Y$ denotes the density of $Y$. Which expression gives a density of $X+Y$?","options":["$\\tfrac{1}{2}f_Y(t)+\\tfrac{1}{2}f_Y(t-1)$","$\\tfrac{1}{2}f_Y(t)+\\tfrac{1}{2}f_Y(t+1)$","$f_Y(t)+f_Y(t-1)$","$\\tfrac{1}{2}f_Y(t)f_Y(t-1)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7e350c1700f08bdba0d31eb8","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_convolution_a8782e74_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"$X\\sim\\operatorname{Pois}(2)$ and $Y\\sim\\operatorname{Bern}(\\tfrac{1}{2})$ are independent. Given $\\mathbb{P}(X=0)=\\mathrm{e}^{-2}$ and $\\mathbb{P}(X=1)=2\\mathrm{e}^{-2}$, what is $\\mathbb{P}(X+Y\\le1)$?","options":["$\\tfrac{3}{2}\\mathrm{e}^{-2}$","$2\\mathrm{e}^{-2}$","$3\\mathrm{e}^{-2}$","$\\tfrac{1}{2}\\mathrm{e}^{-2}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cb229f539dcb908f5a2e6685","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_convolution_a8782e74_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For independent $X,Y\\sim\\operatorname{Exp}(1)$, the sum has density $t\\mathrm{e}^{-t}$ on $t>0$. What is $\\mathbb{P}(X+Y\\le1)$?","options":["$(1-\\mathrm{e}^{-1})^2$","$\\mathrm{e}^{-1}$","$1-2\\mathrm{e}^{-1}$","$1-\\mathrm{e}^{-1}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1940f4be057ca1710c33fb4e","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_convolution_a8782e74_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Independent $X,Y$ have densities $f_X,f_Y$. Using $f_{-Y}(u)=f_Y(-u)$ and $X-Y=X+(-Y)$, which formula gives a density of $X-Y$?","options":["$\\int_{\\mathbb{R}}f_X(x)f_Y(t-x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\int_{\\mathbb{R}}f_X(x)f_Y(x-t)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$f_X(t)-f_Y(t)$","$\\int_{\\mathbb{R}}f_X(x)f_Y(-t-x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c894515c3772440032e75cca","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_convolution_a8782e74_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Independent $X,Y$ have density $\\mathrm{e}^{-x}$ for $x>0$ and zero elsewhere. Which function is the density of $X-Y$ produced by convolution?","options":["$f(t)=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\mathrm{e}^{-|t|}$ for $t\\in\\mathbb{R}$","$f(t)=\\mathrm{e}^{-|t|}$ for $t\\in\\mathbb{R}$","$f(t)=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\mathrm{e}^{-t}$ for $t\\ge0$, and $0$ for $t<0$","$f(t)=\\tfrac{1}{4}\\mathrm{e}^{-|t|/2}$ for $t\\in\\mathbb{R}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ec6f135806d0ae42d2db3cd1","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_convolution_a8782e74_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"$X$ and $Y$ are independent, each with density $2x$ on $(0,1)$. What is $f_{X+Y}(1)$?","options":["$\\tfrac{4}{3}$","$1$","$\\tfrac{1}{3}$","$\\tfrac{2}{3}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"df04e8b51558737f617e240c","time_limit":40}]}}